e). For Minkowski, all timelike curves have infinite length if extended to infinity in the complete flat metric, but here the curve leaves φ(M) at r ∈ ∂φ(M) after finite parameter (if p is at finite affine length along a timelike curve). Can a timelike curve from q ∈ Minkowski reach a boundary point r ∈ ∂φ(M) in finite affine parameter? In Minkowski, a timelike geodesic reaches conformal infinity at infinite affine parameter. But the curve need not be a geodesic of g; it just needs to be timelike w.r.t. g'. Hmm. If g' extends η continuously to ∂φ(M), then a g'-timelike curve from q to p has finite length in g; the part inside φ(M) is a g-timelike curve from q to r with finite g-length. Is there a g-timelike curve of finite Minkowski length from q that does not converge to an interior point? In Minkowski, any sequence with bounded Lorentzian distance from q (timelike-related points) stays in the timelike cone J⁺(q) compactly? Points timelike-related to q within distance L form a compact set (closed bounded region in the cone). So a finite-length timelike curve from q stays in a compact subset of φ(M); hence it cannot approach ∂φ(M) — contradiction. Therefore Minkowski has NO proper future C0 extension. Good: this is a rigorous non-vacuity witness: the class is not vacuous because Minkowski (or any development with complete causal geodesics and a maximal structure) is C0-inextendible. But wait — I should be careful: the "finite length" argument uses g'-timelike curve having finite g-length, which holds if g' is continuous and the curve segment inside φ(M) is timelike with respect to g' restricted = g; but a curve timelike w.r.t. g' need not be smooth — a continuous timelike curve in a continuous metric has a well-defined length if g' is continuous (Lorentzian length of a C¹ curve; timelike curves are usually taken piecewise C¹). So yes, with standard conventions, the argument works using the compactness of the "timelike determination" of a point in Minkowski (the set of points causally related to q with bounded length is compact in the interior). So Minkowski is C0-inextendible: the class has at least one instance satisfying the conclusion, though for the *generic* statement we need a non-meager set of such data.
  
