nside φ(M) from q to r has bounded η-length, and r is in the closure of the timelike determination; the closure of {x : τ(q,x) ≤ L} in R^4 is compact *in the interior chart*; but the curve could leave every compact subset of R^4 while keeping bounded length? In Minkowski, a timelike curve from q with bounded length stays in the compact set K = {x : τ(q,x) ≥ 0, |x-q| ≤ L} (since a timelike curve from q to x has length ≥ Euclidean distance, so |x-q| ≤ L). Thus the portion inside φ(M) is contained in a fixed compact subset of R^4 (identified with φ(M) via φ). If the endpoint r is a limit of points in that compact set, then r corresponds to a point of R^4 under the identification... but r is not in φ(M); the curve's endpoint r is in M' \ φ(M), and the points approaching r are in φ(K). Since φ(K) is compact in M' (continuous image of compact), its limit r lies in φ(K) ⊂ φ(M). Contradiction. So no proper future extension of Minkowski exists. The argument is sound and elementary: it uses g'-continuity to ensure the extension's timelike curve has finite length and the pieces inside are η-timelike.

