My F2b Minkowski argument: let me double-check one subtlety — the extension clause (f) requires some p outside ι(M) lying in I⁺(q;g') for some q ∈ ι(M); I assumed a timelike curve from q to p. I⁺ means p is in the chronological future, so there's a future-directed timelike curve from q to p (by definition of I⁺ in a C⁰ Lorentzian metric — chronological relations are defined via piecewise C¹ timelike curves). The segment inside ι(M) from q to the first exit r is g'-timelike. Since g' = g = η on ι(M) (isometric embedding), it's η-timelike; its η-length is finite (standard convention: timelike curves are rectifiable/absolutely continuous). It stays within the compact η-causal diamond of q with length ≤ L. Then r ∈ closure(ι(K)) = ι(K) (compact), contradiction with r ∉ ι(M). One subtlety: r could be a point where the curve leaves ι(M) but the curve might not have a well-defined "first exit" if it oscillates; since ι(M) is open and the curve is continuous on [0,1] with endpoint p ∉ ι(M), the set {t : γ(t) ∈ ι(M)} is open in [0,1] and contains 0; let t* = sup of the connected component of 0; γ(t*) ∈ ∂ι(M). The segment [0,t*] lies in ι(M) except possibly the endpoint. Length of [0,t*] ≤ total length ≤ finite. ✓ Good.
Another subtlety: is the total g'-length finite? For a timelike curve we may reparametrize by g'-arc length only if rectifiable. A piecewise C¹ timelike curve between two points is rectifiable (the length is finite on compact parameter intervals: length = ∫ sqrt(-g'(γ',γ')) dt with continuous integrand on a compact interval → finite). Since I⁺ relations are witnessed by piecewise C¹ timelike curves, yes. ✓
So the argument is sound under standard conventions. I've flagged the convention. Good.

